PLAN-0014 Wave 4 Round 3 - trust store retest (closes the Round 2 environment item)
utc = 2026-09-28T14:10Z  rustc = rustc 1.94.1  host = Windows NT 10.0.19045.0

ITEM UNDER RETEST
  Round 2 recorded that SDK tests which build a client with the SDK's default HTTPS
  connector can abort with "TrustStore configured to enable native roots but no valid
  root certificates parsed!" and classified it as an execution-environment difference.
  Round 3 was asked to retest it in an environment that can, and not to weaken TLS.

WHAT Round 3 SAW, IN THE ORDER IT HAPPENED
  1. cargo test -p object-storage --all-features           -> exit 101
       log_hygiene::storage_references_never_enter_the_log_stream
       sdk_log_hygiene::turning_every_provider_target_to_trace_does_not_leak
       sdk_log_hygiene::every_configured_case_keeps_storage_references_out_of_the_log_stream
     panicked at aws-smithy-http-client-1.4.0/src/client/tls/rustls_provider.rs:163
       TrustStore configured to enable native roots but no valid root certificates parsed!
     underlying cause, from the SDK's own WARN line:
       native root CA certificate loading errors: [Error { context: "failed to open
       current user certificate store", kind: Os(Os { code: 5, kind: PermissionDenied,
       message: "(access denied)" }) }]
     The three tests touch no code this round changed; they build a real HTTPS client and
     die inside rustls while reading the OS trust store.

  2. Proof it is not a Round 3 regression:
       git stash push -- crates/object-storage/tests/support/mod.rs
                           crates/object-storage/tests/s3_lane_fault_injection.rs
       cargo test -p object-storage --all-features --test log_hygiene   -> same panic, exit 101
       git stash pop
     The code under the stash is exactly HEAD 7fb9f735a7e0a787666d7b62592f08ac5161245a.

  3. Retest with the session able to open the Windows certificate store (the failure above
     is the file-access sandbox denying the current-user certificate store, OS error 5):
       cargo test -p object-storage --all-features --test log_hygiene --test sdk_log_hygiene
       test result: ok. 1 passed; 0 failed          (log_hygiene)
       test result: ok. 4 passed; 0 failed          (sdk_log_hygiene)
       exit 0
     See 31-test-object-storage.log for the same suite green end to end.

  CONCLUSION: an execution-environment restriction on the OS root certificate store, not a
  repository defect and not a Round 3 regression. Nothing was changed to make it pass: no
  certificate verification was disabled, no custom verifier was installed, no dependency
  feature was altered. The tests pass unchanged once the process may read the store.

SUPPORTING MEASUREMENT, taken in the unrestricted session where the tests pass
  Cert:\LocalMachine\Root = 57 certificate(s)
  Cert:\CurrentUser\Root  = 57 certificate(s)
  The store is populated and readable outside the file-access sandbox, which is what the
  diagnosis predicts: the sandbox is denied opening the current-user store (OS error 5),
  the store itself is fine, and no certificate or verifier configuration differs.
SCOPE OF THE RESTRICTION
  The lane under test here is unaffected either way: it talks plain HTTP to a loopback
  endpoint and installs no TLS at all.

